{
  &quot;flashcards&quot;: [
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;What property of the peptide bond restricts rotation around the C-N bond, causing the six atoms of the peptide group to lie in a single plane?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;The partial double-bond character due to resonance.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;What do the angles $\\phi$ (phi) and $\\psi$ (psi) represent in a polypeptide backbone?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;$\\phi$ is the angle of rotation around the bond between the $\\alpha$-carbon and the amino nitrogen, while $\\psi$ is the angle around the bond between the $\\alpha$-carbon and the carbonyl carbon.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;What is the purpose of a Ramachandran plot in structural biology?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;It visualizes the sterically allowed combinations of $\\phi$ and $\\psi$ dihedral angles for amino acid residues in a polypeptide chain.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;Describe the key structural features of a right-handed $\\alpha$-helix.&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;The polypeptide backbone is tightly wound, with R groups protruding outward, and is stabilized by hydrogen bonds between the carbonyl oxygen of residue $n$ and the amide hydrogen of residue $n+4$.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;Why is the amino acid Proline known as an &#39;$\\alpha$-helix breaker&#39;?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;Its rigid cyclic structure restricts the N-C$\\alpha$ bond rotation, and its nitrogen atom in a peptide bond has no hydrogen to contribute to the helix&#39;s hydrogen-bonding pattern.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;In the $\\beta$ conformation, how are the side chains (R groups) of adjacent amino acids oriented relative to the plane of the sheet?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;They extend alternately above and below the plane of the sheet.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;How does the hydrogen bonding pattern differ between parallel and antiparallel $\\beta$ sheets?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;In antiparallel sheets, the H-bonds are linear and thus stronger, whereas in parallel sheets, the H-bonds are angled and thus weaker.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;Which two amino acids are commonly found in $\\beta$ turns, and what are their typical positions?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;Glycine and Proline are common; in Type II turns, Gly is often in position 3 and Pro in position 2 to facilitate the tight turn.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;The fundamental structural unit of $\\alpha$-keratin is the _____, which consists of two intertwined right-handed $\\alpha$-helices.&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;coiled coil&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;What is the characteristic repeating amino acid sequence in collagen, and why is Glycine essential at every third position?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;The sequence is Gly-X-Y, where X is often Pro and Y is often 4-Hyp; Glycine is the only residue small enough to fit in the crowded interior of the three-stranded helix.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;The structure of silk fibroin is predominantly composed of which secondary structure, allowing for its strength and flexibility?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;Stacked antiparallel $\\beta$ sheets, rich in Alanine and Glycine residues.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;What level of protein structure is defined by the overall three-dimensional arrangement of all atoms in a single polypeptide chain?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;Tertiary structure.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;What level of protein structure describes the arrangement of two or more separate polypeptide chains (subunits) in a multisubunit protein complex?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;Quaternary structure.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;What was the key conclusion from Christian Anfinsen&#39;s experiments on the denaturation and renaturation of ribonuclease A?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;The amino acid sequence (primary structure) of a polypeptide chain contains all the information required to fold into its specific, native three-dimensional structure.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;The temperature at which a protein solution&#39;s population is 50% folded (native) and 50% unfolded (denatured) is called the _____.&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;melting temperature ($T_m$)&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;What is the general function of molecular chaperones, such as the Hsp70 family, in a cell?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;They facilitate correct protein folding and help refold misfolded proteins by binding to unfolded regions and preventing aggregation.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;In diseases like Alzheimer&#39;s, misfolded proteins aggregate to form insoluble extracellular deposits known as _____.&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;amyloid fibrils (or plaques)&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;The infectious agent in prion diseases is the misfolded protein PrP$^{Sc}$. How does it propagate infection?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;It acts as a template, inducing the properly folded cellular protein, PrP$^{C}$, to undergo a conformational change into the pathogenic PrP$^{Sc}$ form.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;Which spectroscopic technique is best suited for determining the percentage of $\\alpha$-helix versus $\\beta$-sheet in a protein and for monitoring its unfolding process?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;Circular dichroism (CD) spectroscopy.&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;f&quot;: &quot;What is the term for a compact, locally folded region of a polypeptide chain that often has a specific function, such as a binding site?&quot;,
      &quot;b&quot;: &quot;A domain.&quot;
    }
  ]
